From 8aa9b9f401dca66d824313b724548518702e93a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: NathanNeurotic <109461996+NathanNeurotic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 06:52:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] smbman: a write is complete only for the bytes the server
 says it wrote

smb_WriteAndX() returned the requested byte count for every reply with a
success status, without reading the response's Count field. MS-CIFS
2.2.4.43.2 lets a server answer a write to a full disk with
STATUS_SUCCESS and a Count of zero (the EFBIG and NOSPC rows of its
error table), and a server may store fewer bytes than it was sent and
say so in Count. Against such a server a write cut short was reported to
the caller as complete.

Return the server's Count instead. smb_WriteFile() already advances by
the returned value and stops on a result <= 0, so a short Count now
sends the rest from the right buffer position and file offset, and a
zero Count reaches the caller as write() returning 0. A reply shorter
than the six-word WRITE_ANDX response, a WordCount under 6, or a Count
larger than the request is a failed write (-EIO). CountHigh is not read:
smb_WriteFile() never asks for more than CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE (65535)
bytes in one request.

Servers that report a full disk with an error status, as Samba does,
are unaffected: that reply already returned -EIO.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RwNwRWSWvoz4YfK5AEj37D
---
 iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c b/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
index 4d201bd..588a14a 100644
--- a/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
+++ b/iop/network/smbman/src/smb.c
@@ -1119,7 +1119,17 @@ int smb_WriteAndX(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int
     if ((WRsp->smbH.Eclass | (WRsp->smbH.Ecode << 16)) != STATUS_SUCCESS)
         return -EIO;
 
-    return nbytes;
+    // A success status does not mean every byte was written. MS-CIFS 2.2.4.43.2 lets a server
+    // report a full disk with an error status (caught above) or with success and a Count of zero,
+    // and a server may store less than it was sent. So return the server's Count: smb_WriteFile()
+    // sends the rest from there and stops on a zero. It asks for at most CLIENT_MAX_XFER_SIZE
+    // (65535) bytes, so the 16-bit Count holds the whole answer and CountHigh is not read. A reply
+    // shorter than the six-word response (45 bytes), a WordCount under 6, or a Count over what was
+    // sent is a failed write.
+    if (r < (int)sizeof(WriteAndXResponse_t) || WRsp->smbWordcount < 6 || WRsp->Count > nbytes)
+        return -EIO;
+
+    return WRsp->Count;
 }
 
 int smb_WriteFile(int UID, int TID, int FID, s64 fileoffset, void *writebuf, int nbytes)
-- 
2.43.0

